1. Kinesis Resharding (Split & Merge)

SPLIT SHARD: 1 shard → 2 shards (doubles capacity)
├── When: ProvisionedThroughputExceededException
├── Doubles write capacity (1 MB/s → 2 MB/s total)
├── Parent shard becomes read-only, expires after retention period
└── Cannot split more than 1 shard at a time (sequential)

MERGE SHARDS: 2 shards → 1 shard (halves capacity, saves cost)
├── When: Over-provisioned (low utilization)
├── Both parent shards become read-only
└── New child shard handles all traffic

KEY FACTS:
├── Resharding is NOT instant — takes seconds to minutes
├── UpdateShardCount API: scales to target shard count
├── Can only double or halve at a time (e.g., 4→8→16, not 4→16)
├── During resharding, consumers must handle parent→child transition
└── KCL handles resharding automatically (rebalances workers)

EXAM TRIGGER:
├── "Throughput exceeded" → split shards (or increase shard count)
├── "Reduce streaming costs" → merge underutilized shards
├── "Scale from 10 to 40 shards" → requires multiple resharding operations
└── "Consumer lag during scaling" → KCL auto-handles, but takes time

2. Repartition vs Coalesce (Spark/Glue)

REPARTITION(n):
├── Increases OR decreases partitions
├── Full shuffle (expensive — moves data across network)
├── Use when: INCREASING partitions or need even distribution
├── Example: repartition(100) → 100 evenly-sized output files

COALESCE(n):
├── Only DECREASES partitions (cannot increase)
├── NO shuffle (cheap — just combines existing partitions)
├── Use when: REDUCING partitions (e.g., 1000 small files → 10 larger files)
├── May produce uneven partition sizes
└── Example: coalesce(10) → 10 files (but sizes may vary)

DECISION:
├── Want fewer, larger files? → coalesce (cheaper)
├── Want more files for parallelism? → repartition (must shuffle)
├── Want evenly-sized output files? → repartition (guarantees even)
├── "Reduce small files problem" → coalesce (no shuffle overhead)
└── Writing to S3 with specific file count → repartition(n) or coalesce(n)

GLUE EQUIVALENT:
├── maxRecordsPerFile → controls output file size (Glue-native)
├── groupFiles + groupSize → controls INPUT file grouping (reading)
└── Both repartition and coalesce work in Glue PySpark scripts
